e module docstring, the plan's 'Advisory identity' inference and the change record say one `kind site scope` line per cause. Nothing reads the prose and the suite prefix is reasonable (it separates suites' ids); make the docstring relational ('suite kind site scope') or leave it \u2014 no edit is required for correctness.", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_cache_probes.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "One unreadable snapshot from any project blinds the probe in every repo on the machine", "recommendation": "Scope: none \u2014 deliberate and tested (`test_an_unreadable_snapshot_could_not_look`): `_snapshots` returns None on the first unparseable `*.json` in the shared directory before checking whose `root` it is, so a truncated file from another worktree (crash mid-write, or a non-atomic writer) holds the advisory open everywhere until removed. The NOTE names the path, so it is diagnosable; worth confirming with testament #287 that snapshot writes are atomic (write-then-rename), which is what makes this safe.", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_cache_probes.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "Summary truncation and suite-name fallback are untested", "recommendation": "Scope: instance \u2014 the `_SUMMARY_CAUSES` cap with its '; and N more' tail and the `suite` fallback to 'default' for a non-word suite have no test. Both are display-only (they do not enter the id except the suite fallback, which does: an invalid suite name collapses to 'default' in evidence). A fixture with four causes and one with a bad suite name would pin both.", "files": ["tests/test_testament_cache_probes.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions and alignment (for the owner)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Alignment pass: proceed on the owner's 'build advisory' (2026-10-26). Mid-build decision: the advisory is owner-only (empty recomme